Below is a text description of the structures consistent with the scheme and formulae.

---

## Step 1: Formation of H

Starting material:

\[
\text{2-fluoro-1,3-diaminobenzene}
\]

reacts with two equivalents of dimethyl acetylenedicarboxylate, DMAD:

\[
\mathrm{MeO_2C{-}C{\equiv}C{-}CO_2Me}
\]

Each aniline \(-\mathrm{NH_2}\) undergoes conjugate addition to one DMAD molecule, giving an aminofumarate/enamino diester unit.

Thus **H** is:

\[
\boxed{
\text{2-fluoro-1,3-bis}\left[\mathrm{NH{-}C(CO_2Me){=}CH(CO_2Me)}\right]\text{benzene}
}
\]

Equivalently, H is a 2-fluoro-1,3-phenylenediamine derivative where both amino groups are converted into:

\[
\mathrm{-NH{-}C(CO_2Me){=}CH(CO_2Me)}
\]

This matches:

\[
\mathrm{C_{18}H_{19}FN_2O_8}
\]

---

## Step 2: Thermal cyclization to I

Heating H at \(250^\circ\mathrm{C}\) causes intramolecular cyclization/dehydrative annulation of each anilino-fumarate side chain to form fused heterocycles. The formula change is:

\[
\mathrm{C_{18}H_{19}FN_2O_8 \rightarrow C_{16}H_{11}FN_2O_6}
\]

This corresponds to loss of two molecules of methanol:

\[
\mathrm{H - 2MeOH = I}
\]

Therefore each side forms a cyclic imide/lactam-type ester by condensation with the adjacent aromatic nitrogen system.

The product **I** is the symmetric fluorinated tricyclic core bearing two ester groups, specifically:

\[
\boxed{
\text{dimethyl 9-fluoro-4,6-dioxo-4H,6H-pyrido[3,2,1-ij]quinoline-type dicarboxylate}
}
\]

More practically, it can be described as the doubly cyclized product of H in which both \(-\mathrm{NH{-}C(CO_2Me){=}CH(CO_2Me)}\) groups have cyclized onto the central benzene to give a fused bis-lactam core with two methyl ester substituents.

---

## Step 3: Mitsunobu substitution to J

I is treated with:

- isobutanol,
- DIAD,
- triphenylphosphine.

This is a Mitsunobu reaction. The reactive site is the aryl fluoride. The fluoride is displaced by isobutoxide, giving an isobutoxy derivative.

The formula change is:

\[
\mathrm{I: C_{16}H_{11}FN_2O_6}
\]

to

\[
\mathrm{J: C_{24}H_{27}FN_2O_6}
\]

The increase corresponds to introduction of two isobutyl ester groups if the two methyl esters are transesterified under Mitsunobu conditions. Thus **J** is the diisobutyl ester analogue of I:

\[
\boxed{
\text{diisobutyl fluorinated bis-lactam dicarboxylate core}
}
\]

That is, the two \(-\mathrm{CO_2Me}\) groups of I are converted to:

\[
\mathrm{-CO_2CH_2CH(CH_3)_2}
\]

giving J.

---

## Step 4: Hydrolysis to K

Treatment of J with sodium hydroxide hydrolyses the two ester groups to carboxylates/carboxylic acids.

Thus **K** is the dicarboxylic acid corresponding to J:

\[
\boxed{
\text{fluorinated bis-lactam dicarboxylic acid core}}
\]

with two groups:

\[
\mathrm{-CO_2H}
\]

instead of the ester groups.

---

## Step 5: Coupling with 3-aminopyridine to form ligand L

K is first converted to the corresponding diacid chloride using oxalyl chloride:

\[
\mathrm{-CO_2H \rightarrow -COCl}
\]

Then reaction with 3-aminopyridine gives two amide groups:

\[
\mathrm{-CONH{-}pyridyl}
\]

Because 3-aminopyridine is used, each terminal pyridine is connected through its 3-amino group, leaving the pyridine nitrogen available for Pd coordination.

Thus ligand **L** is the diamide:

\[
\boxed{
\text{fluorinated bis-lactam core bearing two } \mathrm{-CONH(3\text{-}pyridyl)} \text{ groups}
}
\]

or, more explicitly:

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{K\text{-}(COCl)_2 + 2\,H_2N{-}C_5H_4N
\rightarrow
K\text{-}[CONH{-}C_5H_4N]_2}
}
\]

where the pyridine nitrogen atoms are the donor atoms that bind to Pd.

---

# Final structures, summarized

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{H =
2\text{-}fluoro\text{-}1,3\text{-}bis[NH{-}C(CO_2Me){=}CH(CO_2Me)]benzene}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{I =
doubly\ cyclized\ fluorinated\ bis\text{-}lactam\ dimethyl\ dicarboxylate}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{J =
corresponding\ diisobutyl\ dicarboxylate}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{K =
corresponding\ dicarboxylic\ acid}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{L =
corresponding\ bis(3\text{-}pyridylamide),\ i.e.\ } 
\mathrm{core\text{-}[CONH(3\text{-}pyridyl)]_2}
}
\]

CO flow over \(1\ \mathrm{h}=60\ \mathrm{min}\):

\[
V(\mathrm{CO})=0.01\ \mathrm{dm^3\,min^{-1}}\times 60\ \mathrm{min}
=0.60\ \mathrm{dm^3}
\]

Convert to \(\mathrm{m^3}\):

\[
0.60\ \mathrm{dm^3}=6.0\times 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m^3}
\]

Moles of CO passed:

\[
n(\mathrm{CO})=\frac{pV}{RT}
=\frac{101325\times 6.0\times 10^{-4}}{8.314\times 273.15}
\]

\[
n(\mathrm{CO})\approx 2.68\times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{mol}
\]

Since \(70\%\) is converted and \(1\ \mathrm{CO}\) gives \(1\ \mathrm{CO_2}\):

\[
n(\mathrm{CO_2})=0.70\times 2.68\times 10^{-2}
=1.87\times 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{mol}
\]

Number of \(\mathrm{CO_2}\) molecules formed:

\[
N(\mathrm{CO_2})=1.87\times 10^{-2}\times 6.022\times 10^{23}
\approx 1.13\times 10^{22}
\]

Now calculate the number of surface Pd atoms.

Mass of Pd:

\[
m(\mathrm{Pd})=0.10\times 0.10\ \mathrm{g}=0.010\ \mathrm{g}
\]

Moles of Pd:

\[
n(\mathrm{Pd})=\frac{0.010}{106.42}
\approx 9.40\times 10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol}
\]

Total Pd atoms:

\[
N(\mathrm{Pd})=9.40\times 10^{-5}\times 6.022\times 10^{23}
\approx 5.66\times 10^{19}
\]

Only \(10\%\) are surface atoms:

\[
N(\mathrm{Pd,surface})=0.10\times 5.66\times 10^{19}
=5.66\times 10^{18}
\]

Therefore, the number of \(\mathrm{CO_2}\) molecules formed per surface Pd atom is:

\[
\frac{1.13\times 10^{22}}{5.66\times 10^{18}}
\approx 2.0\times 10^3
\]

\[
\boxed{N \approx 2.0\times 10^3\ \text{CO}_2\text{ molecules per surface Pd atom}}
\]
